+_MONTH_NAMES = frozenset(
+    "January February March April May June July August September October November "
+    "December".split())
+# The capitalised words that are never a name: a sentence can start with any of them and
+# `_entities` reads the capital as evidence of one.
+_NUMBER_WORDS = frozenset(
+    "One Two Three Four Five Six Seven Eight Nine Ten Eleven Twelve Thirteen "
+    "Fourteen Fifteen Sixteen Seventeen Eighteen Nineteen Twenty Thirty Forty "
+    "Fifty Sixty Seventy Eighty Ninety Hundred Thousand Million Billion".split())
+
+_NOT_A_NAME = frozenset(
+    "She He They It We You His Her Their Its This That There These Those And But For "
+    "Yet So Nor The A An".split()) | _MONTH_NAMES
+
+
+def _named_entities_only(body: str, unapproved: set, approved: set) -> list:
+    """The unapproved entities that are plausibly NAMES, with fragments rejoined.
+
+    WHY THIS EXISTS, MEASURED. `_entities` reads a capital letter as a name, and across
+    every retained run that flagged anything it produced 22 flags, of which NOT ONE was an
+    invented fact:
+
+        fragment of a longer name   9   Saint, States, Nations, Kingdom, Museum, Modern
+        ALL-CAPS from a source      4   CULT, DEATH, REAL, SATANIC
+        month                       3   August, January, September
+        demonym or adjective        3   Economic, Italian
+        pronoun                     2   She
+        other                       1   Behind
+
+    THE GATE IS NOT LOWERED. An earlier attempt made the whole channel advisory and an
+    adversary showed what that admits: "Dr Helen Marsh of the Wellcome Trust said the
+    programme had failed", with nothing deterministic after Safety. The flaw was never
+    that the gate was too strict -- it is that the instrument cannot tell a name from the
+    word "September". This repairs the instrument and leaves the refusal exactly where it
+    was: `Helen Marsh` and `Wellcome Trust` are rejoined into phrases, remain unlicensed,
+    and still block.
+
+    FRAGMENTS ARE REJOINED, NOT DROPPED. `United Kingdom` reaches `_entities` as two
+    tokens and `Kingdom` alone is meaningless; dropping it would be a loosening. The
+    maximal capitalised run containing it is reconstructed from the prose and judged as
+    one thing -- so a licensed `United Kingdom` stops flagging, and an unlicensed
+    `Wellcome Trust` is flagged once, as itself.
+
+    A SINGLE CAPITALISED WORD IN MID-SENTENCE IS STILL A NAME and still flags: a Writer
+    inventing `Microsoft` has not escaped through this. What stops flagging is a word
+    that is a name only because a sentence began with it, a month, an all-caps heading
+    lifted out of a source, and half of a phrase.
+    """
+    # A PHRASE MAY NOT BEGIN AT A SENTENCE-INITIAL CAPITAL. `_entities` already skips
+    # those as names; the phrase builder has to as well, or it joins the capital that
+    # merely starts a sentence to the name that follows it. Measured on
+    # production-20261001T070632Z: "Thirteen Korean public institutions run a test"
+    # produced the phrase "Thirteen Korean" -- a misfire this repair would have
+    # introduced while removing nine others.
+    # ONLY the leading token is dropped, and only when it cannot be part of a name.
+    # Trimming every sentence-initial phrase was the first attempt and it was worse than
+    # the problem: "Wellcome Trust funded the programme" became the fragment "Trust",
+    # degrading a real catch into the exact shape this repair exists to remove. A number
+    # word or a function word opening a sentence is never part of the name beside it; a
+    # capitalised name is.
+    runs = {}
+    for m in re.finditer(r"\b[A-Z][\w’']*(?:[ ’'-][A-Z][\w’']*)+", body):
+        phrase, at = m.group(0), m.start()
+        head = re.split(r"[ ’'-]", phrase)[0]
+        starts_sentence = (at == 0 or bool(re.match(r"[.!?\n]\s*$", body[:at][-3:])))
+        if starts_sentence and (head in _NOT_A_NAME or head in _NUMBER_WORDS):
+            rest = phrase[len(head):].lstrip(" ’'-")
+            if not rest or not re.match(r"^[A-Z]", rest):
+                continue
+            phrase = rest
+        for part in re.split(r"[ ’'-]", phrase):
+            runs.setdefault(part, set()).add(phrase)
+    out: set = set()
+    for e in unapproved:
+        base = e.rstrip("’'s")
+        if e in _NOT_A_NAME or base in _NOT_A_NAME:
+            continue
+        # An all-caps token is typography -- a source's own headline -- not a name. The
+        # package screen already draws this distinction for title case.
+        if e.isupper() and len(e) > 1:
+            continue
+        phrases = runs.get(e) or runs.get(base) or set()
+        if phrases:
+            # Judge the whole phrase instead. Licensed as a phrase means not flagged.
+            for ph in phrases:
+                if ph not in approved and not all(
+                        p in approved for p in re.split(r"[ ’'-]", ph) if p):
+                    out.add(ph)
+            continue
+        out.add(e)
+    return sorted(out)
+
+
